WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents, episode 48 for May 14, 2026.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.980
Plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.160
Faster pre-training without changing model architecture

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.240
just became practical.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.280
News researches, token superposition training,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.200
cuts wall clock time by up to 2.5 times on models

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.500
from 270 million to 10 billion parameters.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.060
The briefing today centers on a new pre-training method

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.800
that delivers real speed-ups.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.540
Several papers on efficient training and serving

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.580
and fresh agent tools that handle multi-machine control

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.020
and structured evaluation.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.080
News research released token superposition training,

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.340
a two-phase pre-training technique.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.500
It averages contiguous token embeddings into bags

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.220
during the first phase before reverting

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.020
to standard next token prediction.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.500
The method works on dense models from 270 million

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.940
to 3 billion parameters and a 10 billion parameter

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.660
mixture of experts model with 1 billion active parameters.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.240
No architecture, tokenizer, or optimizer changes are required.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.080
It delivers up to 2.5 times wall clock speed-ups

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.980
at matched floating point operations

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.980
while preserving downstream performance.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.200
Teams doing continued pre-training or training

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.040
from scratch on mid-size models

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.080
can cut training time dramatically on the same hardware.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.780
Watch for follow-up work testing whether

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.400
the same bag averaging trick scales cleanly

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.040
past 10 billion parameters.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.960
Three new papers on model efficiency landed as well.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.580
Soma trains a small surrogate model

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.520
on the early turns of a conversation

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.580
to handle the rest of the session.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.780
It uses soft prompts and localized low rank adaptation

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.240
fine-tuning to keep response quality

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.220
while slashing latency and memory

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.080
compared with always concatenating full history

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.180
to a large model.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.480
A simple gate handles one time switching

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.880
with rollback on drift.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.200
Another paper called Freeze Deep, Train Shallow,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.740
shows that deep layers are both critical

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.280
for task execution and highly stable.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.600
The recommendation is to freeze them

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.900
and train only shallow layers during continued pre-training.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.200
This strategy beats full parameter fine-tuning

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.440
on CEVAL and CMM-LU while preserving original knowledge.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.160
The work gives resource constrained teams

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.360
a concrete rule for layer allocation.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.060
A third study tested hidden layer distillation at scale.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.120
It used Gemma 3.4 billion as teacher

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.740
and students up to 735 million parameters

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.800
trained on 168 billion tokens.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.060
The approach produces consistent perplexity gains

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.380
over standard loja-based distillation

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.560
but does not reliably improve downstream tasks.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.520
The results suggest latent signals exist

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.220
but need further breakthroughs to matter in practice.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.140
On the agent side, OpenDesk lets AI agents

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.140
see, click, and type across multiple local machines

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.120
over Wi-Fi.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.560
No cloud or accounts are required.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.780
It works with Claude, cursor, codex, or custom harnesses

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.060
and stays fully encrypted on the local network.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.540
Mac, Linux, and Windows builds are open source.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.740
EVAL agent encodes evaluation expertise

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.140
as composable skills

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.600
and produces complete evaluation artifacts,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.000
including metrics, code, and reports.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.640
It raises first run success from 17.5% to 65%

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.800
and wins 79.5% human preference

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.580
over simple prompting baselines.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.680
The accompanying agent EVAL bench

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.220
and meta-evaluation framework are now available for testing.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.700
Agent Brace splits an LLM agent into a belief state model

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.480
that outputs structured natural language claims

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.200
with verbalized certainty and a policy model

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.620
that acts on that compact belief.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:57.780
It improves performance by 14.5% on twin 2.53 billion

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.440
and 5.3% on twin 3.4 billion

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.900
in partially observable environments

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.100
while keeping context size constant.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.020
Builders also shipped an open source

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.780
one prompt to cinematic real pipeline

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.140
that runs end to end on a single AMD MI 300X.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.220
The pipeline uses flux two-cline, 1.2.2 image to video,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.740
twin 3.5.35 billion as director,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.860
a step music, and cacoro text to speech.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.640
It turns one English sentence into a finished 720p video

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.040
with characters, music, and narration in roughly 10 minutes.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:39.820
All components are Apache 2.0 or MIT

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:41.700
and the full code is public.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.200
A vision critic with 10 structured failure labels

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.080
enables automatic retries for common artifacts.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.320
Check up two action introduces a new 2000 report

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.320
multimodal clinical benchmark.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.160
It tests generation of patient oriented action cards

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.580
that include priority, recommended department, time window,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.660
and safe questions for clinicians.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.140
Experiments reveal clear trade-offs between coverage,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.780
correctness, and safety alignment across general

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.620
and medical large language models.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.300
The data set and evaluation protocol

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.900
are released for further work.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.320
Okay, let's pop the hood on continued pre-training.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.280
Everyone talks about it as a uniform update

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.640
across all parameters.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.920
In practice, layers behave very differently.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.220
Deep layers execute the core task logic

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.280
while remaining highly stable against disruptive changes.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.220
Layer tracer maps this by measuring

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.580
where task execution actually occurs

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.640
and how sensitive each layer is to new gradients.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.640
The result is a clear rule.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.120
Freeze the deep stack and train only the shallow layers.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.180
This consistently beats both full fine tuning

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.260
and the opposite allocation on standard benchmarks.

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:57.560
Deep representations already encode robust general features

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.860
that new data rarely needs to overwrite.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.280
The quality gain is largest on mid-size models

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.580
where full updates are most expensive.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.640
Above roughly 30 billion parameters,

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.500
the relative benefit shrinks because the deep layers

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.000
become even more stable.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.900
When you have limited compute and want to adapt

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.680
a base model to a new domain,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.540
start by freezing everything past layer 70%,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.080
and only tune the early transformer blocks.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.600
The gacha that bites most teams

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.360
is assuming uniform learning rates across depth.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.780
Shallow layers need higher rates

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.560
while deep layers need almost none.

